{
  "title": "Why the sky is blue",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "Hidden colors in sunlight",
      "text": "Sunlight looks white or pale yellow, but it is actually a blend of every color in the rainbow. When all these colors travel together from space, our eyes see them as one bright, colorless beam.",
      "imagePrompt": "A single beam of bright white sunlight splitting through a glass prism into a clear rainbow spectrum of colors."
    },
    {
      "title": "Light travels in waves",
      "text": "Each color travels as a wave of a different size. Red light ripples in long, lazy waves, while blue light moves in tiny, short, choppy waves, much like ripples in a puddle.",
      "imagePrompt": "A side-by-side comparison of a long, smooth red wave and a short, tight, choppy blue wave."
    },
    {
      "title": "Meeting the atmosphere",
      "text": "Before reaching your eyes, sunlight hits Earth's atmosphere, which is packed with tiny gas molecules like nitrogen and oxygen. These particles act like tiny obstacles floating in the air.",
      "imagePrompt": "A beam of light entering Earth's upper atmosphere, which is illustrated with floating, tiny gas molecules."
    },
    {
      "title": "Blue light bounces everywhere",
      "text": "Long red waves glide smoothly past these tiny particles. But short, choppy blue waves crash into them and bounce in every direction across the sky. This scattering fills the entire dome above you with blue light.",
      "imagePrompt": "A short blue wave striking a tiny air molecule and ricocheting outward in multiple directions, while a long red wave passes by undisturbed."
    },
    {
      "title": "Why sunsets look red",
      "text": "At sunset, the sun sits low, so its light travels through a much thicker layer of air. By the time the light reaches you, most blue light has scattered away, leaving only the direct reds and oranges.",
      "imagePrompt": "A diagram showing sunlight traveling a short distance through the atmosphere at noon versus a much longer path at sunset."
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "If you stood on the Moon during the day, where there is no atmosphere, what color would the daytime sky look around the bright sun?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Pitch black",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "feedback": "Correct! Without an atmosphere full of gas molecules, there are no particles to scatter blue light, so space stays completely dark."
      },
      {
        "text": "Deep blue",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "No, a blue sky requires air molecules to scatter the blue wavelengths of light. The Moon has no atmosphere to do this."
      },
      {
        "text": "Bright white",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "No, while the sun itself looks white, the surrounding sky has no particles to reflect or scatter that light toward your eyes."
      }
    ]
  },
  "recap": [
    "Sunlight is made of all colors, each moving in different-sized waves.",
    "Short blue waves collide with gas molecules in our air and scatter.",
    "Without an atmosphere to scatter this blue light, the sky looks black."
  ],
  "goFurther": {
    "overviewCourse": "Physics",
    "relatedQuestions": [
      "Why are clouds white instead of blue?",
      "Why does the ocean look blue?",
      "What causes a rainbow to form after rain?"
    ]
  }
}

